card.statement:
Let $\mathfrak{I}(R^{\Z^n})$ denote the set of ideals of $R^{\Z^n}$ and $\mathfrak{I}(R)^{\Z^n}$ denote the set of $\Z^n$-invariant ideals of $R$. Consider the maps

\begin{align*}
\varepsilon: &\mathfrak{I}(R^{\Z^n})\to \mathfrak{I}(R)^{\Z^n},\quad \nf\mapsto R\nf, \\
\rho:  &\mathfrak{I}(R)^{\Z^n} \to \mathfrak{I}(R^{\Z^n}), \quad J\mapsto R^{\Z^n}\cap J.
\end{align*}

Then $\varepsilon$ and $\rho$ are inverse to eachother and set up an order-preserving bijection between the two sets. In particular, for each $\nf\in\Specm(R^{\Z^n})$, $R\nf$ is maximal among $\Z^n$-invariant ideals of $R$ and conversely, every maximal $\Z^n$-invariant ideal of $R$ equals $R\nf$ for some $\nf\in\Specm(R^{\Z^n})$.
